#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Pre-flight gate for the Nxeon backup runner (stdlib only; runs on every node).

Answers ONE question before every unit of backup work: "is this node quiet
enough that a backup will not be felt by a customer's server?"

  go     - quiet; proceed.
  pause  - busy right now (transient). Wait a minute and ask again; if it stays
           busy past the runner's pause budget the runner stops for tonight and
           resumes next window.
  abort  - the node is in real trouble (memory stalls, swap storm, almost no RAM).
           Stop everything for tonight. Never push a node that is already
           struggling.

It is READ-ONLY: it reads /proc and /sys, nothing else. `--proc-root` redirects
every read to a fixture tree so the decision logic is unit-tested with canned
numbers (test/backup-gate.test.ts) - the live thresholds below were chosen from
the 2026-10-03 baseline of all five nodes (docs: /root/research/backup-design.md
section 5), where the starved nodes (primary, node-4) have swap full and ARC
no-grow but PSI near zero, which is exactly why the gate keys on PSI and swap
ACTIVITY (rate) rather than on swap or free-RAM levels alone.

Output: one JSON object on stdout. Exit code: 0 go, 10 pause, 11 abort, 2 usage.
"""
import argparse
import json
import os
import sys
import time

EXIT_GO, EXIT_PAUSE, EXIT_ABORT = 0, 10, 11

# (name, default, help) - every threshold is overridable with --set name=value
# or NXEON_GATE_<NAME> in the environment, so the owner can tune without code.
THRESHOLDS = {
    "cpu_some_avg10_pause": 30.0,    # % of last 10s some task waited for CPU
